Connect to a Kubernetes Kapsule cluster using the locally installed k9s.
The command downloads the cluster kubeconfig to a temporary file and opens k9s against it.

Use --template to run an arbitrary tool instead: the Go text/template is rendered with
{{.Cluster}}, {{.Region}}, {{.Kubeconfig}} (path to a temporary kubeconfig file, removed when the command exits),
{{.Organization}} and {{.Project}},
and the result is executed as the connect command.

USAGE:
  scw k8s cluster connect <cluster-id ...> [arg=value ...]

EXAMPLES:
  Connect to a cluster with k9s
    scw k8s cluster connect 11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111

  Connect to a cluster with k9s using the pulse view
    scw k8s cluster connect 11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111 template="k9s -c pulses"

  Connect to a cluster in a specific region with k9s
    scw k8s cluster connect 11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111 region=fr-par

  Connect to a cluster with an arbitrary tool via template
    scw k8s cluster connect 11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111 template="kubectl --kubeconfig {{.Kubeconfig}} get pods"

ARGS:
  cluster-id        Cluster ID to connect to
  [region=fr-par]   Region to target. If none is passed will use default region from the config
  [template=k9s]    Go text/template rendered into a command line that replaces the default connect command

FLAGS:
  -h, --help                help for connect
      --list-sub-commands   List all subcommands

GLOBAL FLAGS:
  -B, --beta             Enable beta mode
  -c, --config string    The path to the config file
  -D, --debug            Enable debug mode
  -H, --no-headers       Skip headers in human output
  -o, --output string    Output format: json or human, see 'scw help output' for more info (default "human")
  -p, --profile string   The config profile to use
